===Etymology 2===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*deh₃-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|date}}, from {{der|en|fro|date}}, from {{der|en|LL.|data}}, from {{der|en|la|datus|t=given}}, past participle of {{m|la|dare|t=to give}}; from {{der|en|ine-pro|*deh₃-|t=to give}}. {{doublet|en|data}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# The addition to a [[writing]], [[inscription]], [[coin]], etc., which specifies the [[time]] (especially the day, month, and year) when the writing or inscription was given, executed, or made.
#: {{ux|en|the '''date''' of a letter, of a will, of a deed, of a coin, etc.}}
#: ''[[US]] '''date''' : 05/24/08 = Tuesday, May 24th, 2008. [[UK]] '''date''' : 24/05/08 = Tuesday 24th May 2008.''
#* {{RQ:Dryden Spanish Fryar|passage=And bonds without a '''date''', they say, are void.}}
# A specific day in time at which a [[transaction]] or [[event]] takes place, or is [[appoint]]ed to take place; a given point of time.
#: {{ux|en|the '''date''' for pleading}}
#: The start '''date''' for the festival is September 2.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1844|author=w:Mark Akenside|title=w:The Pleasures of the Imagination|section=Book II
|passage=He at once, Down the long series of eventful time, So fix'd the '''dates''' of being, so disposed To every living soul of every kind The field of motion, and the hour of rest.}}
#: {{ux|en|Do you know the '''date''' of the wedding?}}
#: {{ux|en|We had to change the '''dates''' of the festival because of the flooding.}}
# A [[point]] in time.
#: {{ux|en|You may need that at a later '''date'''.}}
# {{lb|en|rare}} Assigned end; conclusion.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1643|author=w:John Milton|title=Doctrine and Discipline of Divorce
|passage=But because he is but briefe, and these things of great consequence not to be kept obscure, I shall conceave it nothing above my duty either for the difficulty or the censure that may passe thereon, to communicate such thoughts as I also have had, and do offer them now in this generall labour of reformation, to the candid view both of Church and Magistrate; especially because I see it the hope of good men, that those irregular and unspirituall Courts have spun their utmost '''date''' in this Land; and some beter course must now be constituted.}}
#* {{RQ:Pope Rape of the Lock|year=1717|passage=What Time would spare, from Steel receives its '''date'''.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Given or assigned length of life; [[duration]].
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|book=I|canto=IX|stanza=45|passage=Good luck prolonged hath thy '''date'''.}}
#* '''1611-15''', {{w|George Chapman}} (translator), {{w|Homer}} (author), ''{{w|Odyssey|The Odysseys of Homer}}'', Volume 1, Book IV,<sup >[http://www.bartleby.com/111/chapman17.html ]</sup> lines 282–5,
#*: As now Saturnius, through his life's whole '''date''',
#*: Hath Nestor's bliss raised to as steep a state,
#*: Both in his age to keep in peace his house,
#*: And to have children wise and valorous.
# A pre-[[arrange]]d [[meeting]].
#: {{ux|en|I arranged a '''date''' with my Australian business partners.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1903|author=[[Wikipedia:Guy_Wetmore_Carryl|Guy Wetmore Carryl]]|title=The Lieutenant-Governor|publisher=Houghton, Mifflin and Company|page=121
|passage="Why, Mr. ''Nisbet!'' I thought you were in New York."<br>"I had a telegram this morning, calling the '''date''' off,"}}
# One's [[companion]] for social activities or occasions.
#: {{ux|en|I brought Melinda to the wedding as my '''date'''.}}
# {{senseid|en|romantic meeting}} A [[romantic]] [[meeting]] or [[outing]] with a [[lover]] or potential lover, or the person so met.
#: {{ux|en|We really hit it off on the first '''date''', so we decided to meet the week after.}}
#: {{ux|en|The cinema is a popular place to take someone on a '''date'''.}}

